$","$f'(b)g'(b)-f'(a)g'(a)$","$f(b)-g(a)$","$f(b)g(b)-f(a)g(a)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cc183dfd518ab5c0b6395c21","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_parts_86f98312_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x) = x^2$ and $g(x) = \\ln x$ on $[1,2]$. Which term is subtracted in integration by parts for $\\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}(\\ln x)$?","options":["$\\int_1^2 2x\\ln x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_1^2 x^2\\ln x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_1^2 \\frac{2x}{\\ln x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_1^2 \\ln x\\,\\mathrm{d}(\\ln x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b37f0d2d1ee83db5b6ee50d8","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_parts_86f98312_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Using $u=x$ and $\\mathrm{d}v=\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, what is $\\int x\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ transformed into?","options":["$x\\cos x-\\int\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$x\\sin x-\\int\\sin x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\sin x-\\int x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$x\\sin x+\\int x\\sin x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9e930f29aab9f4180deddc7e","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_parts_86f98312_019","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 2x\\cos x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ with $\\int fg'=[fg]-\\int f'g$, which choice simplifies the integral?","options":["$f(x)=2x$ and $g(x)=\\cos x$","$f(x)=\\cos x$ and $g(x)=2x$","$f(x)=2x$ and $g'(x)=\\cos x$","$f(x)=\\cos x$ and $g'(x)=2x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"891bb960b71951a3a4f443b4","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_parts_86f98312_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Using integration by parts, what is $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ with $f(x)=x$ and $g'(x)=1$?","options":["$1+\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$1-\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"48c462e12b5dfafa30a524dc","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_parts_86f98312_006_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The definite integration-by-parts proposition takes $f$ and $g$ differentiable on $[a,b]$. Which hypothesis does it place on $f'$ and $g'$?","options":["They are constant on $[a,b]$.","They are strictly increasing on $[a,b]$.","They vanish at $a$ and at $b$.","They are Riemann integrable on $[a,b]$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"035fb42e8cff7e44bd63095e","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_parts_86f98312_008_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $I=\\int_0^{\\pi}\\mathrm{e}^x\\sin x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, two rounds of integration by parts, with $g'(x)=\\mathrm{e}^x$ in both rounds, give $I=\\mathrm{e}^{\\pi}+1-I$. What follows?","options":["$I=\\tfrac{1}{2}(\\mathrm{e}^{\\pi}+1)$","$I=\\mathrm{e}^{\\pi}+1$","Nothing, since the integral $I$ came back","$I=0$, since $\\sin0=\\sin\\pi=0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5d9afc6b4ae90cb65204bbe4","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_parts_86f98312_012_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Integration by parts on $[\\varepsilon,1]$ with $f(x)=\\ln x$, $g(x)=\\tfrac{x^3}{3}$ gives $\\int_\\varepsilon^1x^2\\ln x\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\dfrac{\\varepsilon^3\\ln\\varepsilon}{3}-\\dfrac{1-\\varepsilon^3}{9}$. What is $\\int_0^1x^2\\ln x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$-\\tfrac{1}{9}$","$\\tfrac{1}{9}$","$+\\infty$","It does not exist"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9b983e1b20cf83064777ca5a","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_parts_86f98312_013_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"basic","question_ty